{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"krull intersection theorem krull intersection theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md","fileName":"Krull Intersection Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Krull Intersection Theorem","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-13 20:10\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Graded Algebra and Hilbert Theory]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Graded Algebra and Hilbert Theory","Jacobson Radical","Artin-Rees Lemma","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"krull intersection theorem krull intersection theorem","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md","fileName":"Krull Intersection Theorem.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Krull Intersection Theorem","heading":"Krull Intersection Theorem","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"For an ideal I in a Noetherian ring and a finitely generated module M, the intersection of all IⁿM is annihilated by an element 1−a with a ∈ I. If I lies in the [[Jacobson Radical]], this intersection is zero.\n\nThe theorem shows that repeated multiplication by a sufficiently small ideal eventually separates every nonzero module element. Its proof uses the [[Artin-Rees Lemma]].","tags":["baby"],"links":["Graded Algebra and Hilbert Theory","Jacobson Radical","Artin-Rees Lemma","commutativealgebra.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Krull Intersection Theorem.md","sourceType":"markdo